<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Baideng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/200-bce">200 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xiongnu-war">Han–Xiongnu War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Baideng took place in 200 BCE near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a> surrounded the Han emperor Gaozu and his army, and the emperor escaped only after negotiations. The episode led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> policy of treaties and payments, confirming the power of the Xiongnu Empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, the Han emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> was still securing his realm against rivals. Judging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Xin of Han too close to the central plain, he moved Xin north to guard the border against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Defeated by the Xiongnu leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>, Xin defected and joined him in taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>, part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. Liu Bang's army recaptured Taiyuan and the places around it, and the enemy withdrew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Han envoys reported that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> soldiers were weak, and an army of 320,000, mostly infantry, prepared to follow them. The adviser Lou Jing warned that a show of weakness could be a ruse; the emperor, angered, had him detained. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> and his vanguard reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>, Xiongnu cavalry surrounded them on Mount Baideng and cut them off from the rest of the Han army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> puts the Xiongnu at over 300,000, while Lev Gumilev argues for 20,000 to 40,000, given the nomads' numbers and the limited pasture.</p><p>The two sides were locked in a stalemate for seven days, and the emperor could not resupply his food. The adviser Chen Ping sent an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>'s wife; what was said is unknown, but she told Modu that taking Han territory did not mean he could live there. Modu was also waiting for two of Xin's generals who failed to arrive, and fearing they had gone over to the Han, he opened a corner of the encirclement and let the emperor's force escape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Once free, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> executed the envoys who had misjudged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and released and promoted Lou Jing. As the raids went on, he took Lou Jing's advice to give a daughter in marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>, later replaced by another woman of the royal clan, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> policy. Government horse pastures were also set up in the northern provinces to build a stronger Han cavalry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Baideng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His confrontation with the newly founded Han Dynasty came to a head in 200 BCE in the <b>Battle of Baideng</b>, near Pingcheng, where Emperor Gaozu was surrounded for seven days by a Xiongnu army that Chinese sources number at 400,000 horsemen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
